Reinstatement, as a derived property, applies to nodes that were once accepted, then defeated, and later restored when their attackers are defeated—the classic pattern: x accepted → a attacks x → b attacks a → x reinstated. Here, x already possesses a defense path of length ≤ k before the addition, so the axiom permits the support-chain addition to activate that path. The axiom does not block this standard reinstatement.

The only problematic sub-case is when the new support chain itself creates a defense that did not previously exist—meaning x had no defense path of length ≤ k before. But that is not reinstatement; it is novel acceptance. Reinstatement presupposes prior acceptance, which requires a pre-existing valid defense path. A node never accepted and lacking any defense path cannot be "reinstated." The critic fails to show this scenario qualifies as legitimate reinstatement.

Furthermore, the axiom targets bounded-depth support-chain additions, not attack-edge additions that defeat attackers. Reinstatement typically works by defeating attackers through attack-edge additions, which are outside the axiom's scope. Thus, the core reinstatement mechanism remains fully derivable. The critic's case conflates newly created defense with reinstatement of prior acceptance, and fails to demonstrate any actual blockage of legitimate reinstatement under grounded semantics.